TECHNICAL BRIEF FOR BROCHURE DESIGN

So we need a promotional brochure for Little Bros. Shell Inc. They've been around forever basically - 95 years in Brentwood, Tennessee. It's a proper auto repair shop, AAA-approved, which is actually a big deal in this business.

The location is 141 Franklin Road, Brentwood, TN 37027. Service department works 7 days a week, 7 AM to 7 PM. Store hours are a bit different - Mon-Sat 6 AM to 9 PM, Sundays 7 AM to 8 PM. People can reach them at info@littlebrosshellinc.com if questions come up.

What they actually do:

Auto repair covers quite a bit - brakes, wheel alignments, axle and CV joint stuff, steering, suspension, transmission, exhaust. Pretty much the full package.

Tire services are huge for them. They carry over 20 major brands - Michelin, Goodyear, Bridgestone, Cooper, Yokohama, Pirelli, the whole lineup. On top of selling tires, they do balancing (both regular and road force), flat repairs, low-profile tire installs, rotations, and TPMS repairs.

Gas station with actual old-fashioned service - someone comes out to your window, checks fluids, cleans windows. Kinda rare these days.

Classic car restoration is another thing they do. They work with customers on budgets, trying to bring old cars back to their original condition.

Preventive maintenance - belts, hoses, cooling system, oil changes, engine diagnostics. State inspections and glass work too.

They've got a small grocery store on site, which is kind of an interesting bonus. Roadside assistance, financing options, shuttle service, pickup/dropoff - all that good stuff.

The brochure format:

Looking at a trifold or bifold that prints on standard letter paper. Should work in full color for digital stuff and grayscale for regular printing. Text needs to be readable - 9pt minimum for body, 12pt for section headers, 18pt for the main headline. Add bleeds and margins, 0.125 inches on all sides at least.

Deliverables should include a print-ready PDF and the source files - PSD, AI, or INDD. Whatever's easier for future tweaks.

Design stuff:

Colors should tie into Shell branding but still feel professional. Red and yellow from Shell logos work as accents, paired with white, gray, or navy for the rest. Nothing too flashy - this is a 95-year-old business, not a startup.

Typography needs to be clean and readable. Sans-serif for body text, something common and properly licensed. Headlines can be a bit more interesting but still professional.

Logo goes in the header or footer of each panel. Make sure it stands out but doesn't overwhelm.

Images should show the service center, cars being worked on, maybe some shots of the team. Professional but approachable.

The overall feel should be established and trustworthy. Not trendy, not aggressive. Just solid and reliable.

Content layout:

Front panel - company name, maybe a tagline about experience, something visual to grab attention.

Services overview - main categories with simple icons or small images. Brief and clear.

Detailed services - go deeper on auto repair, tires, and classic car restoration at least.

Why choose us section - AAA approval, 95 years of service, the tire selection, customer amenities.

Customer amenities - free coffee and WiFi, shuttle, pickup/dropoff, overnight key drop.

Back panel - address, phone, website, email. Leave some space for a stamp or handwritten note.

Key differentiators to play up:

Almost a century in the community. AAA-approved. 20+ tire brands. Full-service approach. Customer-first amenities like free coffee and shuttle.

Branding notes:

This is an established, trusted business. The brochure should feel like that. Avoid anything that screams "we're trying really hard." The company values quick, courteous service - let that come through naturally. Phrases like "Serving Brentwood since 1929" hit the right note. Be careful with Shell branding though - use it appropriately without overstepping.

What to deliver:

Print-ready PDF in CMYK. Source file with organized layers. Separate files for individual panels if it's a multifold. Font files or at least documentation of what fonts were used. Any stock images included should be properly licensed.

File naming: LBShell_Brochure_[Version]_[Date]. Example: LBShell_Brochure_v1_2026.pdf

Send everything via email to the address provided below.

Questions about this brief - just email info@littlebrosshellinc.com

The goal here is a professional, trustworthy piece that accurately represents Little Bros. Shell. Nothing fancy or gimmicky. Just solid work that helps bring in new customers while keeping existing ones happy.
"@
